  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 305 AM EDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nLooking at a nearly picture perfect stretch of weather as we enter \nthe first part of the new work week. Expansive high pressure will \nbuild into the Great Lakes region today, and then slide ESE to the \nmid Atlantic Coast by Tuesday. Meanwhile, unremarkable/dry NW flow \naloft will dominate the upper pattern. Other than a few high clouds \nacross southern and central KY this morning, this early week pattern \nwill yield virtually cloudless skies and light to steady NNE to ENE \nwinds. Will be kind of interesting to see where temps end up given \nthe full sun, but also a somewhat limiting NE surface flow. Is is \ncreating a fairly impressive spread between MAV/MET guidance for \ntoday, and have tended to land in the middle between the milder MAV \nand much cooler MET. This should yield highs mostly in the mid 70s \ntoday and tomorrow. \n\nA very well mixed boundary layer this afternoon will likely yield \nimpressive dry air mix down given mixing heights extending up into a \nnotable hydrolapse above 900mb. Something to be mindful of as we dry \nout from the weekend rain this upcoming work week. \n\nLook for some crisp to down right cool overnight lows as well given \nlight surface winds and cloudless skies. Radiational cooling should \nallow for most min temps in the 45-50 degree range tonight, however \nsome lower 40s will be more common across the NE CWA closer to the \ncenter of the surface high over the OH/PA/WV confluence. Could see \nsome of the coolest sheltered valleys drop into the upper 30s as \nthey completely decouple from a light surface wind. Not sure we will \nget cold enough for any frost concerns, but if so, it would be \nextremely isolated. Unlikely would be a good way to characterize it. \nTuesday night lows will could be a touch milder (46 to 51F for most) \nas the surface high slides east of the area and a very light S \nsurface flow returns by dawn Wednesday. That being said, some low \n40s are still expected for the cooler NE CWA valleys.\n\nWednesday - Sunday...\n\nSlightly warmer than average temperatures and low RH persist \nthroughout the week; especially prevalent Wednesday, Thursday, and \nFriday, we could see max temps in the mid-80s with min dew points \npushing the low-40s. Light surface flow out of the Southwest on Wed \nand the North/Northwest on Thurs as a weak sfc High sits over the \nridge of the Appalachians. A thick mixing layer during the day and \nthe low RH values should prevent the overnight low-level inversion \nfrom creating fog except in low-lying river areas. Overnight lows \nshould remain in the upper 40s and low 50s for \nWednesday/Thursday/Friday nights.\n\nSaturday and Sunday is a bit of a different story, with a potential \nfor our next rain as tropical moisture pushes its way North. While \nthis is still a week out, the last several model runs have been \nconsistent with the moisture, with the ECMWF being the only outlier. \nRainfall totals could see around an inch for the Southern portion of \nthe CWA, with the system creating a sharper gradient of a quarter \ninch for the Southern Indiana portion. As this occurs, mid- to low-\nlevel clouds should set in across the CWA, limiting our decoupling \npotential at night and keeping RH higher and overnight mins in the \nupper-50s and low-60s. Daytime highs are a little muggier with \nhigher daytime dewpoints and temps in the low-80s.",
